window.docContents[238].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-parallelogram-lemma":{"contents":"If x u y v form a 4-cycle in the Hamming cube (i.e. u ≠ v are common\nneighbours of x ≠ y), then u + v = x + y; equivalently the cycle is a\ntwo-dimensional \"face\" x, x + eᵢ, x + eᵢ + eⱼ, x + eⱼ.\n\nThe parallelogram lemma: if u ≠ v are common neighbours of x ≠ y in the\nHamming cube then u + v = x + y./-- The parallelogram lemma: if u ≠ v are common neighbours of x ≠ y in the\nHamming cube then u + v = x + y. -/\nlemma hypercubeHamming_parallelogram {n : ℕ} {x y u v : Fin n → Bool}\n    (hxu : (hypercubeHamming n).Adj x u) (huy : (hypercubeHamming n).Adj u y)\n    (hxv : (hypercubeHamming n).Adj x v) (hvy : (hypercubeHamming n).Adj v y)\n    (hxy : x ≠ y) (huv : u ≠ v) : u + v = x + y := by\n  obtain ⟨i, rfl⟩ := hypercubeHamming_adj_iff_exists.1 hxu\n  obtain ⟨j, rfl⟩ := hypercubeHamming_adj_iff_exists.1 huy\n  obtain ⟨k, rfl⟩ := hypercubeHamming_adj_iff_exists.1 hxv\n  obtain ⟨l, hq⟩ := hypercubeHamming_adj_iff_exists.1 hvy\n  -- cancel the common x: eᵢ + eⱼ = eₖ + eₗ\n  rw [add_assoc, add_assoc] at hq\n  have hkey : e i + e j =\n      (e k + e l : Fin n → Bool) := add_left_cancel hq\n  -- Mathlib classifies the solutions of eᵢ + eⱼ = eₖ + eₗ: either i = k and\n  -- j = l, or i = l and j = k, or i = j and k = l (as true + true = 0)\n  rcases (Pi.single_add_single_eq_single_add_single (by decide) (by decide)).1\n    hkey with ⟨rfl, rfl⟩ | ⟨-, rfl, rfl⟩ | ⟨-, rfl, -⟩\n  · exact absurd rfl huv\n  · abel\n  · exact absurd (by simp [add_assoc]) hxy\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009Subcubes of the Hypercube*","header":"12.8.1. The Parallelogram Lemma","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-parallelogram-lemma"},"/Appendix/#section-chapter-3-constructions":{"contents":"* paleyGraph: Paley graph of a field F.* cayleyS4: Cayley graph of the symmetric group on Fin 4 generated by a 3-cycle\nand a 4-cycle.* SimpleGraph.sigma / Σg: disjoint union of an indexed family of graphs.* natDisjointComplete: disjoint union of complete graphs K[n].* hypercubeHasse: Hasse diagram construction of the n-dimensional hypercube.* hypercubeHamming: Hamming cube definition of the n-dimensional hypercube.* HypercubeVert: recursive definition of the vertex set of the hypercube.* hypercube / Q[n]: recursive definition of the n-dimensional hypercube.* mycielskian / mμ[G]: the Mycielskian of a graph G.* grotzschGraph: Grötzsch graph (i.e. mμ[C[5]]).* mycielskian.left / v ₗ: left copy of a vertex in the Mycielskian.* mycielskian.right / v ᵣ: right copy of a vertex in the Mycielskian.* MycielskianVert / mμᵥ^n V: vertex type of the n-th iterated Mycielskian.* iterMycielskian / mμ^n G: n-th iterated Mycielskian of G.* multiNat: complete multipartite graph with copies of ℕ as classes.* natFinMulti: complete multipartite graph with classes Fin n.* blowUpGraph: blow-up of a graph G by replacing each vertex with a class of\nvertices.* blowUpN / G[n]: n-uniform blow-up of a graph G.* kneserGraph / Kn[n, k]: the Kneser graph — vertices are the k-element subsets\nof β (or of Fin n), with edges joining disjoint distinct subsets.* petersenGraph: the Petersen graph, i.e. Kn[5, 2].\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Definitions","header":"14.2.2. Chapter 3: Constructions","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-3-constructions"},"/Appendix/#section-results":{"contents":"The following key results, theorems, and lemmas are proven throughout this guide and\nits exercises:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix","header":"14.3. Results","id":"/Appendix/#section-results"},"/Constructions/#section-paley-graphs":{"contents":"A special case of a circulant graph is the Paley graph. If F is a finite field, the\nPaley graph of F is the circulant graph on F whose differences are the nonzero\nsquares in F. Usually one also requires |F| ≡ 1 mod 4: then -1 is a square,\nso u - v is a square if and only if v - u is, and each vertex is adjacent to\nexactly half of the others. (For any other finite field the definition below gives\nthe complete graph: if |F| ≡ 3 mod 4 then exactly one of d and -d is a square\nfor each d ≠ 0, while if |F| is even then every element of F is a square.)\nThe circulantGraph calls the constructor fromRel (· - · ∈ s), which\nautomatically removes loops and symmetrizes the relation, so we can simply take all\nthe squares, including 0.\n\n/-- The Paley graph of a field F: distinct elements are adjacent if their\ndifference is a square. -/\nabbrev paleyGraph (F : Type*) [Field F] : SimpleGraph F :=\n  circulantGraph {y | IsSquare y}\n\n\nMathlib's type of integers modulo n is ZMod n. (For n ≠ 0 it is defined to be\nFin n.) If p is prime then ZMod p is a field, so paleyGraph (ZMod p) is the\nPaley graph on p vertices. However, Lean will only find the Field (ZMod p)\ninstance if it can find a proof that p is prime, in the form of an instance of\nFact (Nat.Prime p). Mathlib provides these for p = 2 and p = 3, but for\np = 17 we need to supply one ourselves.\n\ninstance fact_prime_seventeen : Fact (Nat.Prime 17) := ⟨by decide⟩\n\n\n#source ZMod\nexample : SimpleGraph (ZMod 17) := paleyGraph (ZMod 17)\n\n\nThe Paley graph P(17)\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions\u0009Algebraic Constructions\u0009Circulant Graphs","header":"3.2.1.1. Paley Graphs","id":"/Constructions/#section-paley-graphs"},"/Subgraphs/#section-duplication":{"contents":"For a G.Subgraph we don't have access to the many definitions we have already seen\nfor SimpleGraph V such as neighborSet, degree, edgeSet, incidenceSet,\nsupport, map, comap (as well as some we will meet later such as Connected).\nThese have to be defined separately for G.Subgraph and then related back to the\noriginal graph G when needed. This means there is a certain amount of unavoidable\nduplication between the theory of Subgraph and SimpleGraph. We have already met\nSubgraph.support; some of the others are listed below.\n\n#source Subgraph.neighborSet  -- the neighbour set of a vertex in a subgraph\n#source Subgraph.degree       -- the number of neighbours of a vertex in a subgraph\n#source Subgraph.edgeSet      -- the set of edges of a subgraph\n#source Subgraph.incidenceSet -- the set of edges incident to a vertex in a subgraph\n\n\nThe degree of a vertex of a subgraph is at most the degree of that vertex in the\noriginal graph.\n\n#check Subgraph.degree_le\n\n\nThe duplicated definitions are related to each other by bridging lemmas. For example\nSubgraph.coe_degree says that the degree of v in the graph H.coe is its degree\nin the subgraph H. This lets us apply a theorem about degrees in a SimpleGraph to\na subgraph, as we do in the proof of colorable_of_degree_le below.\n\n#check Subgraph.coe_degree -- H.coe.degree v = H.degree ↑v\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs\u0009Subgraphs as Simple Graphs","header":"9.3.3. Duplication","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-duplication"},"/Theorems/#section-zykov-s-theorem":{"contents":"Turán's theorem bounds the number of edges of an (r + 1)-clique-free graph. An edge\nis just a 2-clique, and Zykov proved the following generalization: any\n(r + 1)-clique-free graph on n vertices has at most as many s-cliques as the\nTurán graph T[n, r]. Moreover, except for the degenerate cases of s < 2, s > r\nor s > n, the Turán graph is (up to isomorphism) the unique maximizer.\n\nFor example, the T[7, 3] is 4-clique-free and its parts have sizes 3, 2 and\n2, so it has 3 * 2 * 2 = 12 triangles. Zykov's theorem says that no\n4-clique-free graph on 7 vertices has more triangles than this, and any\n4-clique-free on 7 vertices with this many triangles is isomorphic to T[7, 3].\n\n#eval #(T[7, 3].cliqueFinset 3) -- 12\n\n\nThe proof follows the same basic strategy as Mathlib's proof of Turán's theorem (by\nZykov symmetrization) but we have to work a bit harder.\n\nFix s ≥ 2 and let G have the most s-cliques among the (r + 1)-clique-free\ngraphs on V. We show in turn that G is complete multipartite, that its parts are\nbalanced, and that it has an r-clique unless it is complete. Then, as in Mathlib,\nsuch a graph is isomorphic to T[n, r]\n(Proof of Turán's Theorem). For edges, Mathlib settles each of the\nfirst two steps by comparing degrees; for cliques each needs a new idea.\n\nWe first make \"the most s-cliques\" precise. Mathlib defines IsTuranMaximal r as\nIsExtremal (CliqueFree · (r + 1)), where IsExtremal p says that a graph has\nproperty p and the most edges among the graphs on its vertex type with property\np. To count s-cliques instead of edges, we generalize IsExtremal from edges to\ncopies of any graph H, counted by copyCount. By copyCount_top the copies of\nK[s] are the s-cliques, and an edge is a 2-clique.\n\nG is extremal for copies of H among the graphs with property p if it has\nproperty p and no graph on the same vertex set with property p has more copies\nof H./--\nG is *extremal for copies of H* among the graphs with property p if it has\nproperty p and no graph on the same vertex set with property p has more copies\nof H.\n-/\ndef IsCopyExtremal [Fintype V] {W : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) (H : SimpleGraph W)\n    (p : SimpleGraph V → Prop) : Prop :=\n  p G ∧ ∀ ⦃G' : SimpleGraph V⦄, p G' → G'.copyCount H ≤ G.copyCount H\n\n\nCall G s-clique-maximal for r if it is (r + 1)-clique-free and no\n(r + 1)-clique-free graph on the same vertex set has more s-cliques. So\nIsNCliqueMaximal 2 r is equivalent to IsTuranMaximal r./--\nCall G *s-clique-maximal* for r if it is (r + 1)-clique-free and no\n(r + 1)-clique-free graph on the same vertex set has more s-cliques. So\nIsNCliqueMaximal 2 r is equivalent to IsTuranMaximal r.\n-/\ndef IsNCliqueMaximal [Fintype V] (G : SimpleGraph V) (s r : ℕ) : Prop :=\n  G.IsCopyExtremal K[s] (CliqueFree · (r + 1))\n\n\nIn the proofs we count s-cliques directly, via cliqueFinset, using the following\nreformulation.\n\nlemma isNCliqueMaximal_iff [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    {s r : ℕ} : G.IsNCliqueMaximal s r ↔ G.CliqueFree (r + 1) ∧\n      ∀ ⦃H : SimpleGraph V⦄ [DecidableRel H.Adj], H.CliqueFree (r + 1) →\n        #(H.cliqueFinset s) ≤ #(G.cliqueFinset s) := by\n  refine and_congr_right fun _ => ⟨fun h H _ hH => ?_, fun h H hH => ?_⟩\n  · simpa only [copyCount_top, Fintype.card_fin] using h hH\n  · classical\n    simpa only [copyCount_top, Fintype.card_fin] using h hH\n\n\nAn s-clique-maximal graph has at least as many s-cliques as any\n(r + 1)-clique-free graph on the same vertex set.\n\nlemma IsNCliqueMaximal.card_cliqueFinset_le [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n  [DecidableRel G.Adj] {s r : ℕ} (h : G.IsNCliqueMaximal s r) {H : SimpleGraph V}\n  [DecidableRel H.Adj] (hH : H.CliqueFree (r + 1)) :\n    #(H.cliqueFinset s) ≤ #(G.cliqueFinset s) :=\n  (isNCliqueMaximal_iff.1 h).2 hH\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems","header":"13.3. Zykov's Theorem*","id":"/Theorems/#section-zykov-s-theorem"}});